# name: test/sql/hive/bugs/s3_no_partition_column_statistics.test
# description: S3 - a partitioned Glue table exposes NO statistics for its partition columns, so the
# group: [bugs]
#              optimizer cannot use values we already hold in memory.
#
# DuckDB declines these columns twice over, both deliberately, in MultiFileScanStatsExtended:
#   - :1084-1090 returns nullptr for any hive-partitioning column, because the statistics stored in the
#     data file describe the column the partition value OVERRIDES, and may even have a different type;
#   - :1092-1099 returns nullptr for MULTIPLE_FILES without union_by_name, "we do not want to parse the
#     file metadata for the sole purpose of getting column statistics".
# A partitioned table is always multi-file, so in practice partition columns have no statistics at all.
#
# But the extension already fetched every partition's values into HiveScanInfo::partitions. From the
# PRUNED partition set we can compute EXACT min/max, EXACT distinct count and has-null for a partition
# column -- no file I/O, no Glue call, and exact rather than estimated, because the partition values ARE
# the complete set of values of that column.
#
# Observable used here: the estimated group count of a GROUP BY on the partition column. With an exact
# distinct count the optimizer should estimate ~4 groups for 4 partitions. Today it estimates ~5,056 --
# derived from the (also wrong, see s3_cardinality_ignores_data.test) scan cardinality with no distinct
# count to go on. A hash aggregate sized for 5,056 groups instead of 4 is the visible cost.
#
# Rejected observable, recorded so it is not tried again: a filter on a value outside the partition set
# (WHERE dt = 'zzzz') is NOT a discriminator -- partition pruning already removes every partition, so
# the plan reads ~0 rows today and the assertion would pass without any statistics existing.
#
# Correct behaviour: the group estimate reflects the real number of distinct partition values.

require httpfs

require glue

require-env GLUE_CATALOG_ID

statement ok
attach '{GLUE_CATALOG_ID}' as my_datalake (
    TYPE glue,
    ENDPOINT '{GLUE_ENDPOINT}',
    DEFAULT_LOCATION '{DEFAULT_S3_LOCATION}/{TEST_DIR}'
);

statement ok
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS my_datalake.default.pcol;

statement ok
CREATE TABLE my_datalake.default.pcol (id INTEGER, dt VARCHAR) PARTITIONED BY (dt);

statement ok
INSERT INTO my_datalake.default.pcol SELECT i, 'd' || (i % 4) FROM generate_series(1,4000) t(i);

# exactly four distinct partition values, and Glue knows all four
query II
SELECT (SELECT count(*) FROM glue_partitions('my_datalake.default.pcol')), (SELECT count(DISTINCT dt) FROM my_datalake.default.pcol);
----
4	4

# THE DEFECT: four partitions, so at most four groups. Today the plan estimates ~5,056.
query II
EXPLAIN SELECT dt, count(*) FROM my_datalake.default.pcol GROUP BY dt;
----
physical_plan	<REGEX>:.*Hash Group By.*~[0-9]{1,2} rows.*

statement ok
DROP TABLE my_datalake.default.pcol;
